speechtext:
That the House-do to-morrow go into Committee of the Whole to consider the following proposed resolutions:
Whereas, by chapter 25, of the statutes of 1910, it was provided that the Minister of Railways and Canals might, subject to the terms and conditions therein contained, lease lines of railway connecting with the government railways and operate them as a part of the government railways, but no leases have been made under the said Act:	and
whereas it is deemed advisable to make the provisions hereinafter contained for the leasing of the undermentioned line of railway.. Therefore it is resolved that it is expedient1 that the Minister of Railways and Canals be authorized, with the approval of the Governor in Council, to lease for a term not exceeding 99 rears the following line of railway, namely: -The Yale railway, property of the Acadia

Coal Company, Limited, extending from New Glasgow to Thorburn, in the province of Nova Scotia, a distance of about six miles, upon terms and conditions to be approved of by the government railways managing board; provided, however, that the consideration b.v way of rental for such line of railway shall be an annual rental of one dollar and the carriage of the traffic of the company, its successors and assigns, upon the line of railway to be leased and upon the government railways, and between points to be designated and at rates not in excess of maximum fixed rates for 9uch carriage between such points, such fixed rates to be subject to a provision for revision of such rates at the end of each period of 10 years during the continuation of the lease.
Such railway so leased shall become and be operated as a part of the government railway.
And the following:
Whereas, by chapter 25, of the statutes of 1910, it was provided that the Minister of Railways and Canals might subject to tin terms and conditions therein contained, lease lines of railway connecting with the government railways and operate them as a part of the government railways but no leases have been made under the said Act, and whereas it is deemed advisable to make the provisions hereinafter contained for the leasing of certain of such lines of railway. Therefore it is resolved, that it is expedient that the Minister of Railways and Canals be authorized, with the approval of the Governor in Council, to lease for a term not exceeding 99 years, any of the following lines of railway, namely:
The Quebeo Oriental railway, extending from Matapedia to Paspebiac, a distance of about one hundred miles;
The Hampton and St. Martins railway, extending from Hampton to St. Martins, a distance of about thirty miles;
The Albert railway, extending from Salisbury to Albert, a distance of about forty-five miles;
The Moncton and Buctouche railway, extending from Moncton to Buctouche, a distance of about twenty-eight miles;
The Caraquet railway and Gulf Shore railway, together extending from Bathurst to Tracadie, a distance of about eiglity-five miles;
The York and Carleton railway, extending from Cros9 Creek to and beyond Stanley, a distance of about ten miles;
The International railway, extending from Campbellton to the St. John valley at St. Leonards, a distance of about one hundred and thirteen miles;
The Cape Breton railway, extending from Point Tupper to St. Peters, a distance of about thirty miles;
The Temiscouata railway, extending fron Riviere du Loup to Connors, a distance of about one hundred and thirteen miles;
The New Brunswick and Prince Edward Island railway, extending from Sackville Junction to Cape Tormenting, a distance of about thirty miles;
The Elgin and Havelock railway, extending from Elgin to Havelock, a distance of about twenty-eight miles;
The Kent Northern railway, extending from Kent Junction to Richibucto, a distance of about twenty-seven miles;
Including the company's rolling stock in each case.
Upon terms and conditions to be approved of by the Government Railways Managing Board, provided, however, that the rental to be paid for each such line of railway shall in no case exceed an amount to be determined in the following manner, that is to say, the value of the railway proposed to be leased shall be fixed and determined by the judge of the Exchequer ^ Court of Canada, upon the report of the chief engineer of the government railways, based upon the examination of the railway by, and the report of the engineer appointed by the Department of Railways and Canals to inspect such railway during construction, such value also to include interest during construction, the cost of equipping the railway, the cost of terminals and right of way of the railway now in use in the operation of the said railway in any city or incorporated town, (the amount of such interest during construction, the co9t of equipping the railway, the cost of terminals and of right of way of the railway in any city or incorporated town to be fixed by such judge upon proper and sufficient evidence) and deducting therefrom any aid received from the government of Canada towards the construction of such railway, and also deducting such amount, if any, as may, in the opinion of the said chief engineer, be necessary to put any such line of railway in a safe and efficient condition.
Except that in the case of railways which have, since their construction, either through sale, foreclosure proceedings, transfer of shares of stock or securities, become either wholly or substantially the property of other companies or persons, at a cost less than the original cost thereof with the deduction aforesaid, then the value of such last mentioned railways shall for rental purposes hereunder, be determined by such judge upon evidence of, and shall be based upon the amounts actually paid by the present owners for such line of railway, shares and securities, respectively. Such judge shall, in the case of the railways last above mentioned, make a report showing the amounts pai I for such railways and their rolling stock, shares and securities, respectively, and the owners thereof, as of date of April 1, Mil. After receiving the report and finding of such judge, the Minister of Railways may, with the approval of the Governor in Council, lease any of said lines of railway and pay therefor, for the first five years of the term of the lease, an amount as rental not exceeding three per cent per annum on such value so fixed by such judge; for the next five years 0f the term of the lease an amount not exceeding three and one-half per cent per annum on such value; and thereafter during the term of the lease an amount not exceeding four per cent per annum on such value, such rental to be paid half-yearly.
Nothing herein shall be deemed to require the taking over of any of the said railways if, in the opinion of the Governor in Council, having regard to the convenience of the public or the interests of the Intercolonial railway, the terms and conditions proposed by the company are not fair and reasonable.
Any such line so leased shall become and be operated as part of the government railways.

He said:	His Excellency the Governor
General having been apprised of the subject matter of these resolutions.' recommends them to the favourable consideration of the House.
